module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.checker_change_landing_subject_keyed_to_changed_files

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data checker_change_landing_subject_keyed_to_changed_files: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "checker_change_landing_subject_keyed_to_changed_files" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a change to the CHECKER lands, and the required floor's prepared subject is keyed to the files the change touched rather than to the verdicts it decides, so a module the new checker refuses is never re-judged and main admits it red** (THE INVALID STATE is a merged tree holding a module that refuses under Strict preparation, while every required lane that ran on the change that made it refuse reported green. A module's Strict verdict has two inputs: its own .dag closure and the checker that reads it. Every seed ground of v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed was keyed to the first. The host classified a changed non-.dag path as structural-empty for the .dag frontier, which is true of the frontier and false of the verdict. So an edit to the seed's resolve, infer or typecheck reached no module at all, however many verdicts it changed.)",

    "THE HARM is deferred and misattributed, as in [[changed_declaration_signature_consumer_unplanned]]. The refused module sits on main until an unrelated later change pulls it into that change's own subject. That change's author meets a refusal in a file they did not touch, caused by a checker edit they did not make, and every PR in between merges over a red it cannot see. It is the ordinary compiler floor (DESIGN section 4b: values inhabit declared types) failing silently on the merge path.",

    "SPECIMEN (2026-09-30, dashboard node adhoc-3ce90efb-5a2). gunbc#12441 (7299157) added to the seed's infer (src/v1/stage0/src/v1_compiler_infer.rs, and its authority src/v1/04_infer.dag) a check that refuses a distinct type argument of one generic coproduct at a declared return. v2.test.claim.type_param_binder_frame returned Outcome<Node> where Outcome<ResolvedTree> was declared, at two functions. It had been prepared GREEN on gunbc#12432's own landing run 36585500472, so it was not broken by its last .dag edit. #12441's landing run 36641740799 judged 2342 of 7028 admitted modules, and this module was not among them. The refusal first surfaced on gunbc#12628's runs (36662347539 and after), where changed-witness selection pulled the module in, and gunbc#12756 repairs it. MISREAD ON THE WAY, kept so the next reader does not repeat it: the brief read the required run's admitted-minus-judged line, then printed as `unresolved-module-identities`, as a bucket of silently dropped resolve failures. It is the 4685 modules outside the subject, none of them handed to the checker, and the line now prints `outside-subject-module-count`.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS. The victim has no diff and no .dag dependency on anything the change touched, so neither direction of the changed-interface class reaches it: not the touched-entry seed, and not the reverse-dependent seed of [[changed_declaration_signature_consumer_unplanned]]. The changed file is not a .dag file. The module is correct under the old checker, and the refusal exists only under the new one. It is not the composition of two PRs: the break is fully present in one change's landing revision.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: below 1 on the merge path. The refusal was real and fired whenever the module was prepared, but the required path had no edge from the checker to any module. CEILING 2, mechanically preventable. The edge is decidable: the checker's inputs are the files its binary was compiled from, which cargo records in dep-info. Rung 3 would need every landing to prepare every module by construction, which is the whole-corpus floor, a cost question rather than a missing fact.",

    "REPAIR AND ITS BOUND, LANDED WITH THIS ROW. v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed checker_subject_rule reads the running checker's dep-info record (observed by v1_compiler.cli_run.checker_dependency, never a hand list) and the run's changed paths. A changed checker input seeds every admitted module into Strict preparation under SeedCheckerSourceChanged, and the run announces rule=EveryAdmittedModule with the changed checker paths. An unreadable, empty or foreign record refuses on CI. Claims are planned as before, and only preparation widens. THE BOUND: the observed grain is the whole binary, so an edit to the interpreter, which decides claim verdicts and not typecheck verdicts, widens preparation too. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: a per-verdict dependency record, meaning the set of checker source items the Strict resolve/typecheck path reaches, sufficient for a checker edit outside that set to leave the subject narrow while one inside it still seeds every module. MEASURED AND WITHHELD (2026-09-30): applying the widening cost 2142 s and 43.66 GiB peak on srv1 over main with gunbc#12765, over the 28 GiB per-PR class and the 30-minute line. So on the required path the rule is decided and announced, and the widening is withheld under the standing drop that already owns this loss, gunbc.rung_drop.compiler_change_refusals_land_outside_every_compiled_closure, by the declared standing v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed checker_widening_standing. The amendment gunbc.rung_drop_amendment.checker_edge_withheld_over_the_per_pr_runner_class records the measurement and the 34 blocking diagnostics in 14 files that the widened run found already on main. A second drop was not declared: the class was already dropped, and the prior-art search that missed it was keyed to this directory alone.",

    "DECLARED FRONTIER, THE SAME CLASS ONE LEVEL DEEPER: claim verdicts. The observed roster is the whole claim_executor record, so it also covers the evaluator and runtime that execute claims. A change there can flip a claim verdict in a module outside the subject, and this repair widens only preparation: claims are still planned from the changed witnesses, so a runtime edit re-typechecks every module and re-runs none of their claims. TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: a per-verdict dependency record that separates the Strict typecheck path of the checker binary from its claim-execution path, sufficient for a runtime-only edit to plan exactly the claims whose evaluation reaches the edited items, measured on the fleet before enabling.",

    "THE UNDECIDED REST STAYS A COUNTED FRONTIER. Modules outside a run's prepared subject are counted on every required run as `outside-subject-module-count`, and have no judged standing until the standing registry of [[admitted_module_without_judged_standing]] exists. That count is measurement, not a wall. A committed roster of them would be a ratchet wearing a wall's name."
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed", decl_name: "checker_subject_rule", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed", decl_name: "CheckerSubjectRule", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed", decl_name: "checker_widening_standing", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.rung_drop_amendment.checker_edge_withheld_over_the_per_pr_runner_class", decl_name: "checker_edge_withheld_over_the_per_pr_runner_class", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed_test", decl_name: "a_checker_edit_seeds_every_admitted_module", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed_test", decl_name: "a_change_outside_the_checker_keeps_the_narrow_subject", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.floor_subject_seed_test", decl_name: "an_unobservable_checker_refuses_rather_than_narrowing_or_widening", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
